  by: agent  # 396a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 396a
  clock: 1  # 396a
  nonce: 396a  # 396a
  data:  # 396a
    kind: invariant  # 396a
    title: "A catalogued primitive whose call site is not a function application emits no matchable edge: haskell zero-arg IO actions and java's System.in attribute are rowed but unreachable"  # 396a
    status: violated  # 396a
    priority: 1  # 396a
    parent:  # 396a
    tags:  # 396a
    - catalogue  # 396a
    - haskell  # 396a
    - java  # 396a
    - analysis_quality  # 396a
    - io_boundary  # 396a
    isbefore: []  # 396a
    duplicate_of: []  # 396a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 396a
    pr_ref:  # 396a
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-01 while measuring WI-lipis's haskell and java halves. Found the\nway a dead control is always found: the CONTROL produced no finding, and the\ncontrol is supposed to be the thing that cannot fail.\n\nTHE SHAPE. Every catalogue is keyed on a callee name, and the analyzers emit a\n`calls` edge for a function APPLICATION. A primitive whose call site is not an\napplication -- a zero-argument IO action, or an attribute read -- is rowed but\nemits no matchable edge, so the row is unreachable no matter how it is\nclassified. Rowed is not found (WI-pimun's lesson in a second form).\n\nINSTANCE 1 -- HASKELL ZERO-ARGUMENT IO ACTIONS. Cold survey, one file:\n\n    s <- getLine                      -> NO call edge at all\n    s <- readProcess \"uname\" [] \"\"    -> haskell:external:0-0:readProcess\n\nBoth are rowed ipc_recv under Prelude / System.Process. The discriminator is\napplication-with-arguments, not the row. So Prelude's entire zero-argument\nipc_recv set -- getLine, getChar, getContents, readLn -- is undetectable.\nVerified end-to-end in one cold verify-claims run over the same file: the\n`readProcess` control reaches `writeFile` and returns VIOLATED, while the\n`getLine` function with the identical flow shape produces nothing.\n\nINSTANCE 2 -- JAVA'S ONLY ipc_recv ROW IS AN ATTRIBUTE.\n\n    java.yaml ipc_recv:  module java.lang.System, attributes [in]\n\n    int c = System.in.read();  ->  java:external:0-0:in.read:external_symbol\n\nThe receiver is not resolved to `java.lang.System`, so the attribute row never\nmatches. Consequence: java has ZERO reachable untrusted_input sources through\nipc_recv. (db_read and net_recv DO fire -- `java.sql.ResultSet.getString` and\n`java.net.Socket.getInputStream` both reach a sink in a cold run -- so this is\nspecific to the attribute row, not to java taint generally.)\n\nPOPULATION.\n    haskell getLine/getChar/getContents/readLn    27 sites, 8 repositories\n      (simplex-chat 6, cohort6_crypto 6, pandoc 5, yesod 3, stack 3)\n    java System.in                                not counted; it is one row,\n      but it is the ONLY ipc_recv row java has.\n\nThe haskell count is small. The reason to file anyway is not volume: it is that\nthese are the CANONICAL untrusted-input calls in their languages, they are the\ncontrols any future measurement will reach for, and a dead control reads as\nconfirmation of whatever the measurer already believes. This one already cost a\nretraction once (WI-lipis's first rust probe).\n\nRELATION TO INV-muvis, which must not be assumed to cover it. INV-muvis is about\nthese rows being declared `fs_read` when they should be `ipc_recv`, and haskell's\nPrelude row has SINCE been moved to ipc_recv -- so INV-muvis's catalogue half\nlooks done for haskell while the calls remain just as undetectable. Fixing a row\ndoes not make it reachable. That is the whole point of filing this separately.\n\nTWO INSTANCES, ONE FAMILY, CAUSES NOT ESTABLISHED AS IDENTICAL. A zero-argument\napplication and an attribute read are different constructs and may need\ndifferent fixes. Do not fix one and assume the other -- INV-zuvib's filed first\nstep, for the same reason.\n\nREPRO ARTEFACT: scratchpad lipis4/{hs2,java2,java3,java_ctrl}/"  # 396a
    fields:  # 396a
      statement: "Every catalogued primitive must be reachable by some call site the analyzers emit an edge for. VIOLATED: haskell 'getLine' (and getChar/getContents/readLn) emit no call edge at all, while 'readProcess x y z' in the same file does; java's System.in emits java:external:0-0:in.read rather than resolving java.lang.System, so java's only ipc_recv row never matches and java has no reachable untrusted_input source through ipc_recv."  # 396a
      root_cause: "The analyzers emit a calls edge for a function APPLICATION and the catalogues are keyed on the callee of one. A zero-argument IO action bound with '<-' is a bare identifier, not an application, so haskell emits nothing; an attribute read is a field access, not a call, so java emits an unresolved receiver-qualified name. The row's boundary is therefore irrelevant -- correcting the classification, as INV-muvis did for haskell's Prelude rows, leaves the call just as undetectable."  # 396a
- op: discuss  # e68a
  at: '2026-09-01T05:45:40Z'  # e68a
  by: agent  # e68a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e68a
  clock: 2  # e68a
  nonce: e68a  # e68a
  message: "CORRECTION + ENRICHMENT from the module-key concept audit, 2026-09-01 (cadence).\nFull write-up: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-module-key_09012026.md\n\nTHE JAVA HALF'S STATED ROOT CAUSE IS WRONG IN ITS SPECIFICS, and the correction\nmakes it fixable. This item says java \"emits java:external:0-0:in.read rather than\nresolving java.lang.System\" and that \"an attribute read is a field access, not a\ncall, so java emits an unresolved receiver-qualified name\". Measured on a\npurpose-built probe against the shipped analyzer, java emits:\n\n    module_attr_ref  dst=java:System:0-0:System.in:external_symbol   name=System.in\n    module_attr_ref  dst=java:System:0-0:System.out:external_symbol  name=System.out\n\nThree corrections:\n  1. The MODULE SLOT DOES RESOLVE. It holds `System`, which _module_matches\n     matches against the catalogue's java.lang.System via the suffix arm.\n  2. AN EDGE IS EMITTED AND IT REACHES THE PIPELINE. The edge type is\n     module_attr_ref, which IS a member of tag_io_boundaries' call_types\n     (it was added there deliberately -- see the comment at that set).\n  3. The failure is SOLELY that the NAME slot is module-qualified. The catalogue\n     rows module=java.lang.System, name=in; the edge offers name=System.in.\n\nTwo-line differential through the production classifier, same edge, name slot the\nonly difference:\n\n    name=\"System.in\"  -> None\n    name=\"in\"         -> IoPrimitive(boundary='ipc_recv', module='java.lang.System',\n                                     name='in', kind='attribute')\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM'S SCOPE. The java half is NOT the\n\"not-a-function-application\" shape this item names -- it is INV-januj (filed as\npython-only: \"python puts a module-qualified name in the NAME slot\"). The two\nitems are ONE defect in two languages. Measured cross-language: 3,515 refs\nre-state the qualifier, 299 miss a row the bare name would hit, across java 57 /\npython 116 / bash 43 / go 39 / javascript 25 / c 18 / typescript 1, and 58 of\nthose are boundaries not otherwise tagged in the same repo (java System.out x51,\nSystem.err x3, python datetime.now x4 -- a LOWER bound, the dedup key was coarse).\n\nTHE HASKELL HALF IS UNTOUCHED BY THIS. haskell's zero-arg getLine/getChar/\ngetContents/readLn genuinely emit no edge at all, which is a different mechanism\nfrom java's. This correction narrows the item rather than closing any of it:\nDON'T FIX ONE AND ASSUME THE OTHER.\n\nWHY IT LOOKED LIKE ONE SHAPE. Both halves present identically at the finding level\n-- java's only ipc_recv row and haskell's zero-arg reads both produce nothing --\nand \"no finding\" is the same observation whatever produced it. That is the same\ntrap this item was itself found by (a dead control), reached one level down."  # e68a
- op: discuss  # 7c82
  at: '2026-09-01T07:14:14Z'  # 7c82
  by: agent  # 7c82
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7c82
  clock: 3  # 7c82
  nonce: 7c82  # 7c82
  message: "JAVA HALF FIXED in PR #684 (dev 6cca459732) -- via INV-januj, not via this item's\nstated mechanism. ITEM STAYS VIOLATED on its haskell half.\n\nThe java half was never the \"call site is not a function application\" shape this\nitem names. The shipped analyzer emits\n`java:System:0-0:System.in:external_symbol` through a `module_attr_ref` edge that\nIS in `tag_io_boundaries`' `call_types`: the module slot resolves, the edge\nreaches the pipeline, and only the NAME slot was at fault (it carried\n`System.in` where java.yaml keys `in`). Verified now on dev through the\nproduction classifier:\n\n    classify_call_in_catalog(module_path=\"System\", name=\"System.in\")\n      -> IoPrimitive(boundary='ipc_recv', module='java.lang.System',\n                     name='in', kind='attribute')\n\nSo java's only ipc_recv row is reachable, and java has a live ipc_recv control\nagain -- which is one of the two things blocking WI-lipis's java arm.\n\nTHE HASKELL HALF IS UNTOUCHED AND IS THE REAL INSTANCE OF THIS ITEM'S STATEMENT.\nZero-arg `getLine` / `getChar` / `getContents` / `readLn` bound with `<-` are bare\nidentifiers, not applications, and haskell emits no edge for them at all -- a\ndifferent mechanism from java's, needing a different fix. DON'T FIX ONE AND ASSUME\nTHE OTHER, which is what the java half's misdiagnosis here amounted to: both\nhalves present identically at the finding level (nothing found), and \"no finding\"\nis the same observation whatever produced it.\n\nRecommend re-scoping this item's statement to haskell when it is next touched."  # 7c82
