  set:  # 1182
    title: "A haskell zero-argument IO action emits no call edge at all, so Prelude's getLine/getChar/getContents/readLn are rowed but unreachable (java half FIXED via INV-januj)"  # 1182
    fields:  # 1182
      statement: "Every catalogued primitive must be reachable by some call site the analyzers emit an edge for. VIOLATED, HASKELL ONLY as of 2026-09-02: a zero-argument IO action (getLine, getChar, getContents, readLn) emits NO call edge at all, while an application with arguments in the same file does. Verified cold on dev c05d573e69: a file whose main does 's <- getLine' then writeFile produces 1 io edge (fs_write) and no ipc_recv, while the same shape with 'readProcess \"uname\" [] \"\"' produces 2 (fs_write + subprocess). At the edge level getLine emits nothing and readProcess emits haskell:external:0-0:readProcess. The JAVA HALF THIS ITEM WAS FILED WITH IS FIXED and is recorded in the discussion; java System.in.read() now fires ipc_recv."  # 1182
      root_cause: "The analyzers emit a calls edge for a function APPLICATION and the catalogues are keyed on the callee of one. A zero-argument IO action is a bare identifier in every position it appears -- bound with '<-', chained with '>>=' or '=<<', lifted with '<$>', or discarded with 'void' -- so haskell emits nothing for it. The row's boundary is therefore irrelevant: correcting the classification, as INV-muvis did for haskell's Prelude rows, leaves the call just as undetectable. NOTE THE FILED '<-' FRAMING IS TOO NARROW -- the measured call sites use five spellings, and a scan for '<-' alone finds 9 of 25."  # 1182
  remove:  # 1182
    tags: [java]  # 1182
- op: discuss  # 9e2b
  at: '2026-09-02T03:14:43Z'  # 9e2b
  by: agent  # 9e2b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9e2b
  clock: 5  # 9e2b
  nonce: 9e2b  # 9e2b
  message: "RE-SCOPED TO HASKELL, both halves re-verified live on dev c05d573e69 (cold\nXDG_CACHE_HOME per case). Doing what the previous entry said to do \"when this\nitem is next touched\", with the measurement rather than on its say-so.\n\nJAVA HALF -- CONFIRMED FIXED, WITH A CONTROL IN THE SAME RUN.\n\n    System.in.read() then Files.writeString   -> 2 io edges: fs_write + ipc_recv\n    Socket.getInputStream().read() (control)  -> 2 io edges: fs_write + net_recv\n\nSo java's only ipc_recv row is reachable and the control discriminates.\n\n*** ONE THING A FUTURE READER MUST NOT MISREAD, and it is why this is spelled\nout rather than just marked fixed. THE EXACT EDGE THIS ITEM FILED AS THE DEFECT\nIS STILL EMITTED TODAY. *** The java_in survey carries BOTH:\n\n    calls           -> java:external:0-0:in.read:external_symbol      <- filed as the bug\n    module_attr_ref -> java:System:0-0:System.in:external_symbol      <- the one that fires\n\nThe filed edge was never the load-bearing one. Anyone who re-runs this item's\noriginal repro, greps for `java:external:0-0:in.read`, and finds it will\nconclude the fix regressed. It has not: the boundary rides the module_attr_ref\nedge, which resolves its module slot and matches java.lang.System. Recorded\nbecause \"the filed evidence still reproduces\" and \"the defect is still live\"\nare different facts and this item is a case where they diverge.\n\nHASKELL HALF -- STILL VIOLATED, same run, same discipline.\n\n    s <- getLine ; writeFile ...              -> 1 io edge: fs_write only\n    s <- readProcess \"uname\" [] \"\" ; writeFile -> 2 io edges: fs_write + subprocess\n\nAt the edge level, which is where the mechanism is visible:\n\n    hs_getline  calls -> haskell:external:0-0:writeFile     (the SINK only)\n    hs_ctrl     calls -> haskell:external:0-0:readProcess   (the control fires)\n                calls -> haskell:external:0-0:writeFile\n\ngetLine emits NOTHING. The discriminator is application-with-arguments, exactly\nas filed.\n\nMINOR DRIFT IN THE CONTROL, noted so it is not read as a change in this item:\nreadProcess now classifies `subprocess`, where this item recorded it as\nipc_recv under System.Process. The catalogue moved; the discriminator did not.\n\nPOPULATION RE-MEASURED: 25 call sites across 9 repositories (filed: 27 across\n8). THE FILED FIGURE REPRODUCES -- this is a confirmation, not a correction.\n\n  cohort6_crypto 6, simplex-chat 6, stack 3, yesod 3, pandoc 2, purescript 2,\n  haskell-language-server 1, shellcheck 1, xmonad 1\n\n  Counting rule, stated because three different rules give three answers:\n  occurrences of the four names in .hs files, EXCLUDING line-start definitions,\n  export-list continuations, `import ... hiding (...)` lines and comments. The\n  unfiltered count is 33 and includes pandoc's export lists; a `<-`-only scan\n  gives 9.\n\n  I ran the `<-`-only scan FIRST and was about to file \"the filed population is\n  overcounted 3.7x\" off it. It is not -- `<-` is one of five spellings the real\n  call sites use, and the scan was the wrong instrument, not the number wrong.\n  Recorded so the next person does not repeat the same wrong scan and reach the\n  same wrong correction.\n\nTHE FILED '<-' FRAMING IS TOO NARROW, which is the one substantive change to\nthis item's content. A zero-arg action is a bare identifier in EVERY position,\nand the measured sites use five spellings:\n\n    l <- getLine                                    binding\n    T.hPutStrLn h =<< T.getLine                     reverse bind\n    T.getContents >>= mdToRST >>= T.putStrLn        forward bind\n    contents <- lines <$> getContents               lifted into a functor\n    race_ (forever $ void getLine) . forever $ do   discarded via void\n\nThe last accounts for 8 of the 25 (one line duplicated across repositories), so\nthe effective distinct-shape count is smaller than 25 and the per-repo spread is\nwhat matters. A fix keyed on `<-` would leave the majority standing.\n\nALSO OBSERVED, NOT COUNTED: several sites are QUALIFIED (`T.getLine`,\n`BS.getContents`, `B.getContents` -- Data.Text.IO / ByteString), which are\ndifferent catalogue rows from Prelude's and were not in this item's scope.\nWhether they share the mechanism is UNMEASURED. Do not assume they do; that is\nthis item's own standing instruction applied to itself.\n\nSTILL P1, and the reason is unchanged and is not volume: 25 sites is small, but\nthese are the CANONICAL untrusted-input calls in the language, they are the\ncontrol any future haskell measurement reaches for, and a dead control reads as\nconfirmation of whatever the measurer already believed."  # 9e2b
